{
  "id": "all-ancestors-of-a-node",
  "questionId": "all-ancestors-of-a-node",
  "slug": "all-ancestors-of-a-node",
  "title": "All Ancestors of a Node",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Topological sort",
  "patterns": [
    "topological sort",
    "graph",
    "dag",
    "reverse graph"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "an ancestor of node X is any node that can reach X by following directed edges - what single graph operation finds 'everything that can reach a target'?",
    "reverse the direction of every edge; now finding ancestors of X in the original graph is the same as finding everything reachable FROM X in the reversed graph",
    "run a DFS or BFS from node X on the reversed adjacency list, marking every node you visit",
    "collect all visited nodes except X itself, sort them ascending, and return them"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "including the target node itself in the ancestor list",
    "traversing the original graph forward instead of reversing edges first, which finds descendants instead of ancestors",
    "not deduplicating nodes reached via multiple converging paths, e.g. using a list instead of a visited set"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a directed acyclic graph (DAG) with `n` nodes, labeled 0 to n - 1, and a list of directed edges, where each edge `[u, v]` means there is an edge from node `u` to node `v`. You're also given a target node.\n\n**A node `u` is an ancestor of node `v` if there's a directed path from `u` to `v` following one or more edges. Return every ancestor of the target node, in ascending order.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= n <= 1000",
      "each edge [u, v] has 0 <= u, v < n and u != v",
      "the graph has no cycles and no duplicate edges",
      "0 <= node < n"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "the target node has no ancestors at all (nothing points to it)",
      "several converging paths reach the target - each ancestor should be counted only once",
      "an empty edge list",
      "the target node is at the end of a long chain of dependencies"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 5, edges = [[0, 1], [0, 2], [1, 3], [2, 3], [3, 4]], node = 4",
        "output": "[0, 1, 2, 3]",
        "expectedOutput": "[0, 1, 2, 3]",
        "explanation": "every other node has a directed path reaching node 4"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 4, edges = [[0, 1], [1, 2], [2, 3]], node = 0",
        "output": "[]",
        "expectedOutput": "[]",
        "explanation": "nothing points toward node 0, so it has no ancestors"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 5, edges = [[0, 2], [1, 2], [2, 3], [3, 4]], node = 3",
        "output": "[0, 1, 2]",
        "expectedOutput": "[0, 1, 2]",
        "explanation": "nodes 0, 1, and 2 all have a path reaching node 3; node 4 does not (the edge goes from 3 to 4, not the other way)"
      }
    ],
